{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"0fa0a4f3c56ea5e75152a6ff0833f52ce1a8ab6d419c8cd629c2dbd93fccecaf","packTokens":5324,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":1850,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"taste","tokens":1054,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"field-ideas","tokens":416,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"reqs","tokens":1756,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":16,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"design"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nYou list the screens of a product for Google Stitch to draw, one prompt per screen.\n- One screen per page a user sees: ids S-1, S-2, ..., each with its route, a proposed file path and the requirement ids it serves in \"reqs\".\n- Every requirement is on a screen, or in \"noScreen\" with the reason it has no page of its own.\n- Each \"prompt\" describes that page alone, in its normal state (its other states are drawn apart, from \"states\"): its purpose, its sections in order, and real sample content from the product's domain (names, amounts, dates). No lorem ipsum, no generic names, nothing the requirements do not ask for.\n- \"links\" lists every way from this page to another screen: its menu items and the buttons and links that open one, each with the exact words on the control (\"label\") and the screen id it opens (\"to\"). A screen has one label, the same on every page that links to it; a button that stays on its page is not listed. The prompt uses those same words for those controls. The pages one kind of user sees share one menu: the same items, labels and order on each of them, and never a page meant for another kind of user.\n- Where a page shows something its user knows by sight (a product, a place, a person, a vehicle), the prompt asks for a real photograph of it, with what is in the picture; never an illustration, an icon in its place or a grey box.\n- \"field-ideas\", when given, tells how the leading real products of this field arrange their pages. Where a requirement fits one of their patterns, the prompt asks for that component by what it is and what it holds (a boarding-pass strip with the route set large, a balance in large tabular figures above the latest movements), with this product's own content. Never a product's name, logo or wording, and never a colour from that list: the design system sets the colours.\n- \"client-references\", when given, are the client's own references with their pictures (R-1, R-2, ...). Stitch never sees them, so the prompt says in words what is taken: for a \"layout\" or \"match\" reference, the navigation and the regions in the order its picture shows, on the screens it relates to; for an \"inspire\" reference, its feel (how dense, how much white, how large the pictures and headings). The requirements beat a reference.\n- \"shells\" lists the frame each kind of user's pages share, one per kind (ids such as \"employee\", \"admin\"). Its \"header\" says in two or three sentences what the header holds, the same on every page: the brand's words as written, where the menu sits (beside the brand or centred), and what is on the right (the signed-in user's name and role, say; whether their picture is a photograph or initials, and on which side of the name it sits), and the brand's mark in one phrase (a small square in the accent with a white \"P\", or the words alone). Each screen names its shell in \"shell\"; a page outside any (sign-in) leaves it out. Every prompt of a shell is given that header word for word, so a prompt never describes the header itself.\n- \"sample\" is the product's sample data, the same on every page: each person with their name, department and role, what the pages show of them (their space, their vehicle and its plate), the place's one time zone, and the counts more than one page shows (capacity per floor, totals), all agreeing with each other, and, when any page lists events (a log, a history, a report, a check), one dated list of them (when, who, what, where, the result) that those pages all take their rows from, with counts that add up to it. Every prompt takes its names, figures and dates from it and never contradicts it; one person is never two people's photograph.\n- Labels, headings and messages use the plain words a user of this product would say (\"Give up my space today\", \"3 spaces free\"), never system or legal wording (\"relinquish\", \"allocation governance\", \"materialize\"). Each page has one primary action; one that cannot be undone says so beside its button.\n- \"title\" is the page's one heading. A page the menu opens has its menu label as its title, word for word; a menu label names a place (\"My space\", \"Free spaces\"), never an action, in three words at most.\n- A page never repeats its menu as a panel of links or tiles. A row that opens a record is itself the link, or has one control that does; no checkbox or radio on a row unless the page acts on several rows at once. Filters apply as they are chosen, with no Apply button. A page shows no field, id, switch, building, gate or device that neither the requirements nor \"sample\" name.\n- \"phone\": true on the pages a person opens on a phone on the move (the main tasks of the largest group of users), five at most; each is drawn a second time at phone width.\n- Each prompt composes its page like a finished product, not a wireframe: one thing the eye meets first (named, with its size against the rest), then the sections in the order the user needs them, what sits beside what on a wide screen, and where the page is left empty on purpose. Give real figures, names and dates enough to fill every list and table with believable rows.\n- \"taste\" holds the layout rules, the anti-patterns and the component replacements the design system is written by. Compose each page by them: where a page would have a component from the left column of its table, the prompt asks for the one on the right, with this product's content (one headline figure with its context above a dense table, never four cards of figures with sparklines; a list or a table, never a grid of near-equal tiles; no row of three equal cards). Its lines about colours, fonts, shadows, motion and CSS are the design system's and stay out of a prompt, and its lines about heroes, pricing, testimonials and footers apply to a public marketing page alone. A page outside any shell that holds one task (sign in, a confirmation, a message that access is refused) is not a hero: its heading, its one sentence and its one action sit in a single panel no wider than 440 px, centred on the page both ways, with the brand's mark and words above the panel and nothing else on the page (no header bar, no footer, no office or place name). The requirements, and a rule above, win over it.\n- \"states\" lists the page's extra states besides its normal page (empty, loading, error, success, validation) only where the requirements need them.\n- \"brand\" lists the client's colours (hex) and fonts only when the requirements or references name them; otherwise leave it empty.\n\n### 6. Define Layout Principles\n- No overlapping elements — every element occupies its own clear spatial zone. No absolute-positioned content stacking\n- Centered Hero sections are BANNED when variance exceeds 4 — force Split Screen, Left-Aligned, or Asymmetric Whitespace\n- The generic \"3 equal cards horizontally\" feature row is BANNED — use 2-column Zig-Zag, asymmetric grid, or horizontal scroll\n- CSS Grid over Flexbox math — never use `calc()` percentage hacks\n- Contain layouts using max-width constraints (e.g., 1400px centered)\n- Full-height sections must use `min-h-[100dvh]` — never `h-screen` (iOS Safari catastrophic jump)\n\n### 9. List Anti-Patterns (AI Tells)\nEncode these as explicit \"NEVER DO\" rules in the DESIGN.md:\n- No emojis anywhere\n- No `Inter` font\n- No generic serif fonts (`Times New Roman`, `Georgia`, `Garamond`) — distinctive modern serifs only if needed\n- No pure black (`#000000`)\n- No neon/outer glow shadows\n- No oversaturated accents\n- No excessive gradient text on large headers\n- No custom mouse cursors\n- No overlapping elements — clean spatial separation always\n- No 3-column equal card layouts\n- No generic names (\"John Doe\", \"Acme\", \"Nexus\")\n- No fake round numbers (`99.99%`, `50%`)\n- No AI copywriting clichés (\"Elevate\", \"Seamless\", \"Unleash\", \"Next-Gen\")\n- No filler UI text: \"Scroll to explore\", \"Swipe down\", scroll arrows, bouncing chevrons\n- No broken Unsplash links — use `picsum.photos` or SVG avatars\n- No centered Hero sections (for high-variance projects)\n\n## 12. Anti-Vibe-Coded Components (extends §5 and §9)\n\nThese are the components that make an app look generated. For each, the DESIGN.md must specify the substitute.\n\n| AI-default component | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| Pill badge above hero (\"New: AI-powered ✨\") | Nothing, or one dated plain-text line in mono (e.g. \"v2.4 — 3 Oct\") |\n| Feature cards: icon in rounded square + title + 2 lines | Numbered editorial list with real product crops, or annotated screenshot callouts |\n| \"Trusted by\" grayscale logo strip | One specific quote with full name, role and context — or omit |\n| Stats row \"10K+ / 99.9% / 24/7\" | One real metric with its source and timeframe |\n| 3-tier pricing with highlighted \"Most Popular\" middle | Plan comparison table with honest limits, or a single plan with usage-based detail |\n| Testimonial carousel with stars and circular avatars | 1–2 long quotes, set typographically large, no stars |\n| Gradient mesh / blob / glow backgrounds | Flat surface; structure through hairline rules, type scale and spacing |\n| Glassmorphism cards (`backdrop-blur` + white/10) | Solid surfaces with a defined border or a tint step |\n| Bento grid of near-equal tiles | Only when content genuinely differs in size; otherwise a list or table |\n| Dashboard: 4 KPI cards each with sparkline and green \"+12%\" | One headline metric with context, then a dense, sortable table |\n| Hero split: headline left, floating phone/3D render right | The product's actual UI, cropped tight to the interesting part |\n| Sparkle/wand icon for anything \"AI\" | A label that says what the feature does |\n| \"Get started\" / \"Start for free\" CTA | A product-specific verb (\"Plan your first route\", \"Import a spreadsheet\") |\n| Default shadcn look (zinc, 0.5rem radius, ring) on everything | A deliberate radius system (see §13) and a custom focus style |\n| Icon on every list item and nav link | Icons only where they speed up recognition; text labels carry meaning |\n| Mega footer with 4 link columns + newsletter box | Compact footer sized to the real number of links |\n| Uniform section template repeated down the page | Vary section rhythm: full-bleed, narrow text column, dense table, quote |","tools":[],"user":"How the leading products of this field arrange their pages:\nDeveloper and SaaS tools:\n- GitHub: plain bar, soft corners; dark or white, green merge button, system font, tight tables\n- Linear: plain bar, soft corners; near-black or white, one muted indigo, very dense lists\n- Vercel: plain bar, sharp corners; pure black and white, hairlines, no colour\n- Notion: plain bar, soft corners; warm white, black text, serif option, almost no colour\n- Atlassian: plain bar, soft corners; blue on white with status lozenges\n- Datadog: plain bar, soft corners; purple brand, dense charts on white or dark\nWhat their pages share: Neutrals first (white or a deliberate dark), one cool accent for the primary action, hairline borders, small type and compact tables. Colour appears for status (running, failed) and in charts. These tools are dense on purpose; avoid hero banners and large cards.\n\nEvents and ticketing:\n- Ticketmaster: plain bar, soft corners; blue actions on white, seat map, countdown\n- Eventbrite: plain bar, round corners; orange-red on white, image cards\n- Meetup: plain bar, round corners; red on white, people and group photos\n- SeatGeek: plain bar, soft corners; bright cyan with dark navy, deal scores\nWhat their pages share: Image-led event cards with date, place and price, one bright action colour, a seat or ticket picker with a clear total, and a ticket detail with a code. Organiser screens are tables and counts of sales and check-ins.\n\n<artifact id=\"reqs\" kind=\"requirements\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Feedlot web app shall provide email-and-password sign-in for administrator-managed accounts without self-service registration or SSO, allow Administrators to manage accounts and assign Administrator or Operator roles, and display controls and pages defined for each assigned role.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an Administrator starts synchronization, the Feedlot web app shall request procurement and receiving records from the configured mock source and display returned records, source identifiers, per-record synchronization outcomes, and errors.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Feedlot web app shall display PO-line-to-lot-to-pen traceability, available procurement and animal fields, pen capacity and current animal counts, and lot purchase-cost allocations proportional to assigned received head counts.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens a lot lifecycle view, the Feedlot web app shall display target weight, lifecycle milestones, projected market date, and the growth assumption used, using measured ADG when available and otherwise the configured yard expected daily gain.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Feedlot web app shall allow users to view and enter feed inventory item fields, retain entered units for display, show normalized calculation values where applicable, and display opening, received, consumed, and closing stock values.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens inventory projections, the Feedlot web app shall display estimates for daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, six-month, and annual horizons using the configured wastage ratio in estimates and projections, without displaying a separate wastage stock movement.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Feedlot web app shall allow users to create and view rations with ingredient proportions, nutritional values, intended animal or weight range, and calculated cost per pound using normalized calculation units while retaining entered units for display.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user submits nutrition or animal-range constraints for ration optimization, the Feedlot web app shall display candidate ingredient mixes with costs and trade-offs and require an explicit save or assign action before a candidate is saved, assigned, or used.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user generates a dated pen feeding plan, the Feedlot web app shall display planned quantity as the bunk rate in pounds of as-fed ration per animal per day multiplied by planned animal count, with plan details and separate lot allocations proportional to current lot animal counts in the pen.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-10\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens pen feeding schedules, the Feedlot web app shall show plans by delivery date and pen with planned quantity and Planned, In progress, Completed, or Skipped status.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-11\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an Operator records a feeding activity, the Feedlot web app shall allow an activity with or without a matching plan, submit the activity details and lot allocations, and display the returned planned and actual quantities, variance when linked to a plan, inventory result, feed cost, energy, feeding status, lot allocations, and lot rollups.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-12\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a feeding activity is accepted, the Feedlot web app shall display the inventory consumption and lot allocations returned by the API without asserting a web-side inventory decrement formula.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-13\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a feeding save is refused for insufficient stock, the Feedlot web app shall retain entered activity values and display the returned error without presenting the activity as saved or displaying updated inventory or lot totals.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-14\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an authorized user edits or cancels a saved feeding activity, the Feedlot web app shall display the returned activity state, inventory totals, and lot totals, or display the returned error if the API refuses the change.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-15\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an Operator records a lot weight measurement, the Feedlot web app shall submit the dated measurement fields and display the returned measurement in the lot's dated weight history and trend.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-16\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens lot performance, the Feedlot web app shall display returned days on feed, total weight gain, ADG, total as-fed feed consumed, FCR, feed cost of gain, and projected ship date, and refresh those values after an accepted feeding or weight event.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-17\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens lot financial performance and insights, the Feedlot web app shall display feed cost of gain separately, available purchase and feed costs and recorded sale revenue, label broader ROI unavailable when required data is missing, and display returned feeding or sale-timing recommendations.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-18\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens the operations dashboard, the Feedlot web app shall display active lots and pens, operational counts and inventory, ration and plan information, feed and weight performance, energy use, and lot rankings.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-19\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens the yard summary, the Feedlot web app shall display aggregate metrics across active lots, including total head, average ADG, average cost of gain, and total feed cost.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-20\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens the dedicated Feed-to-Gain report, the Feedlot web app shall display lot comparisons for ADG, FCR, and cost of gain and provide date-range, breed, sex, and pen filters, with sex filtering limited to known values.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-21\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user exports the Feed-to-Gain report, the Feedlot web app shall provide the displayed or filtered report data as a CSV or PDF file.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-22\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a user opens the demo and presentation view, the Feedlot web app shall show mock operational data and materials covering the proposed solution and architecture diagrams, demo flow, coding prompts, validation, challenges, production readiness, and go-to-market analysis.\"\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\nList every screen and write one Stitch prompt for each."}